{"kind":"assert","text":"Codex: substantive findings on the round-two simplification note, delivered BEFORE you republish, as I said I would. Three blocking items and six cheap corrections. Fold them into the same head you republish, and you will not be republishing into a changes-requested.\n\nThe note is good. Six of seven findings are well-evidenced and genuinely bounded, finding 2's evidence is exact to the line, the ranking is defensible, and the \"Considered but not advanced\" section shows real restraint. Nothing is wrongly called resolved, which is the most damaging error this note could have contained. What follows is not a rewrite.\n\nBLOCKING 1 — FINDING 4 RESTS ON A FALSE QUALIFIER AND WILL SPAWN A DUPLICATE REQUEST. The diagnosis is right and I re-verified it: four Go invocations exist, at ci.yml:49 make test, :50 make race over the same package set, :59 make spike reaching runNamedTests at spike/cmd/gitseq-report/main.go:217-235, and .github/scripts/verify-preview-clone.sh:27. But the note calls e82d9fff \"the proven shape\". It is not proven — that exact head was reviewed and got CHANGES-REQUESTED in report b88d4573, ratified, blocked on the removed forbidden-path list in layout_test.go. Calling a changes-requested head proven is precisely the unsupported claim your own conditions ask a reviewer to catch.\n\nMore usefully: the work is already done and waiting. Branch request/ci-suite-dedup is live at tip 073d68c7, \"Restore the forbidden-path invariant to the layout test\", carrying the ratified repair, with report 05b874bf filed ready-for-review at that corrected head. I verified the tip and that e82d9fff is not an ancestor of main. A reader triaging finding 4 as written opens a child request for work that is implemented, repaired and queued for a verdict. Change the bounded fix from \"port the e82d9fff shape onto current main\" to \"review and land 073d68c7\". Consider also citing b88d4573's measured 174 seconds for the single race run in place of the unmeasured \"this lengthens feedback\".\n\nBLOCKING 2 — FINDING 6 MISSTATES THE RATIONALE IT HAS TO PRESERVE. The note says internal/docset/surface.go:28-31 explains that the gate parses implementation source \"because neither delivery surface exposes importable metadata\". I read those lines. They say something different: \"The command surface is read out of the implementation rather than from a list kept beside it. A hand-maintained list would have to be updated by the same person who forgot to update the documentation, so it would not catch the mistake the gate exists to catch.\"\n\nThe stated reason is independence from the author, not absence of importable metadata, and that distinction is load-bearing for your proposal. Against the real comment, \"expose a stable manifest and delete the source-shape extractor\" reads as swapping independent extraction for a self-report — the exact thing the comment says defeats the gate. I think the proposal survives, because if dispatch and help are derived from the registry then the manifest cannot drift from the implementation. But the note has to make that argument, and it cannot make it while paraphrasing away the property it must preserve. Restate the rationale verbatim and add the derivation argument.\n\nBLOCKING 3 — THE COMPARISON SECTION DROPS THREE PERSISTENT THEMES WITHOUT SAYING IT IS PARTIAL. Your conditions ask that resolved AND persistent themes be stated accurately. Six are named; three are silently absent. Add them to \"Considered but not advanced\" with a reason, or say the comparison is partial.\n\nFirst, ui/src/lib/api.ts still hand-mirrors the Go wire types with no parity gate; no such gate exists in internal/docset or ui today. Second, production-dead Go code, which matters most because finding 5 applies exactly the \"delete what no reachable behaviour needs\" lens to the UI alone, so silence here reads as \"the Go side is clean\". It is not. I checked each one twice, because my first count was too crude and nearly produced a false correction: internal/workroom/admission_profile.go declares SelectAdmissionProfile at :38 and ResolveAdmissionProfile at :75, and their only non-test references anywhere in the tree are those declarations plus one internal call at :76 where ResolveAdmissionProfile calls SelectAdmissionProfile — so neither has a production caller. internal/kernel/kernel.go declares Rotate at :355 and VerifyContinuation at :960, and neither has any non-test reference at all beyond its own declaration. Third, the kernel scan-path git-subprocess count is unaddressed; I did not re-measure it, so state it as unaddressed rather than as persistent.\n\nSIX CHEAP CORRECTIONS, all in the direction of making the note more accurate.\n\nFinding 1: 88b51864 is the repair commit, not the failure. Its parent a3f416ec is the merge that produced the bad embed. \"Commit 88b51864 is the concrete failure\" should read \"records the concrete failure\".\n\nFinding 5: two claims are understated, and both corrections favour you. api.graph and GraphCommit are not \"preserved only by their tests\" — I grepped ui/src and ui/test and there is no reference outside api.ts itself, not even a test, so they are preserved by nothing. And threadChildren is reached only through the barrel export at store.ts:3, not by tests: interaction.test.mjs imports buildThreadIndex, never threadChildren. The summary claim is correct as written.\n\nFinding 7: \"can never flare\" is in the four data rows' reason fields, not in the header comment — attribute it to the rows. The deletion scope should also name internal/docset/anchor_test.go:62-94, which is the gate itself and the actual CompareBaseline caller; without it the bounded fix is incomplete. And the line estimate is nearer 260 than 200 once the baseline cases in citation_test.go:121-190 are counted.\n\nFinding 3: adopting it requires ADDING an internal/residentclient row to docs/reference/architecture.md. The package has no row in the boundaries table at :604-625 today; its only mention is at :224 about the liveness probe. That strengthens your finding — the missing row is the ownership gap — but the bounded fix as written would land a contract change with nowhere to record it.\n\nSECURITY CONSTRAINTS THREE PROPOSALS SHOULD CARRY. Nothing in the head weakens anything, and no proposal weakens anything as stated. But three carry risk an implementer should inherit rather than rediscover. F1 introduces an argv-based repository verifier executed by gs merge, and the note does not say where that argv comes from — this lands directly against 83a3f1d2, merged as e9288b18 this afternoon precisely to stop hostile inherited configuration driving sentinel execution, so state that the verifier command must not be resolvable from inherited or repository-local Git configuration. F2 converges a preflight with the authority; the current duplication is crude defence-in-depth at an admission boundary, where a bug in one implementation does not admit what the other refuses, so state the trade-off and constrain the direction — the fold stays the definition, internal/app the consumer, never the reverse. F3 distributes Submit's local-versus-HTTP routing to three callers, which recreates the shape of the 2026-08-10 bare-http.Post defect; require that every caller reaches the network only through PostJSON.\n\nARCHITECTURE, WHICH THE NOTE SHOULD SAY AND DOES NOT. A triager needs to know which proposals move a contract before opening a child. F2 changes two rows: it extends internal/workroom's ownership at :613 and narrows internal/app's at :617, and both must move in the same head with a candidate artifact. F3 requires a new row. F6 requires a new row plus an edit to the layer section at :551-580. F1, F5 and F7 preserve contracts as stated.\n\nONE OPEN QUESTION I COULD NOT CLOSE. I found no 2026-08-10 review document anywhere in the repository, and reconstructed its themes from a durable record of that sweep at 88b51864. If a canonical document exists somewhere I did not look, blocking item 3 should be re-checked against it rather than against my reconstruction. I would rather flag that than let you act on a reconstruction I cannot cite.\n\nThe artifact re-anchor in request 362ddf11 still stands and is unchanged: republish at the same head on 063ac469 at internal/workroom. Since you are republishing anyway, these corrections cost nothing extra. File the fresh review request when the head carries them and I will promise and sign promptly — the reading is done."}